"""Original teaching cases. All organisations and workload numbers are illustrative."""
CHAPTERS=[]
def chapter(title,goal,concept,nodes,caption,case,choices,failure,task,answer,checks,refs):
 CHAPTERS.append(dict(title=title,goal=goal,concept=concept,nodes=nodes,caption=caption,case=case,choices=choices,failure=failure,task=task,answer=answer,checks=checks,refs=refs))

chapter('Architecture starts with a decision',
'Connect a technical choice to a user need, a constraint and a consequence.',
['Software architecture is the set of important structural decisions that shape how a system changes and operates. It includes responsibilities, dependencies, data ownership and deployment boundaries. A framework name describes only part of that picture. Two applications using FastAPI can have very different architectures.',
'Begin with a concrete journey. A shopper chooses an item, submits an order and waits for payment confirmation. Ask what must remain true at each step. Stock must not become negative; the same payment attempt must not charge twice; an uncertain payment must not be shown as a confirmed failure.',
'Constraints narrow the choices. A team of three with one operational owner should value a system it can diagnose and restore. A large budget does not remove coordination costs. Record assumptions, alternatives and the evidence that would cause you to revisit a choice. Architecture is a continuing decision process.'],
['User journey','Constraints','Decision','Evidence'],
'Read left to right: a decision should be traceable to the problem it serves.',
'Soko Market is our fictional Tanzanian marketplace. Three developers maintain a mobile-friendly storefront, stock records and orders. Payment confirmation arrives from an external provider and can be delayed. The first release serves one warehouse. A developer proposes six services because the company hopes to grow. The architect asks which current problem requires six independent deployments. No team needs that independence yet, and local stock/order transactions would become harder.',
[['One application','Fast local changes','Shared release and runtime'],['Six services','Independent deployments','Network and operational work'],['Start modular','Clear boundaries now','Requires dependency discipline']],
'The wrong optimisation is to design for an imaginary future while missing today\'s recovery needs. Soko could ship many containers yet still have no tested backup. Count the work required to operate each option, not only the amount of code needed to create it.',
'Write a five-line decision record for Soko\'s first release: context, options, decision, consequence and review trigger. Include one reason your preferred choice could later become unsuitable.',
['A defensible decision is one modular application with one relational database and a background worker. Orders, inventory and payments have explicit interfaces. This fits the current team and keeps local invariants manageable. The worker handles slow provider interactions outside the browser request.',
'The consequence is a shared release and some shared failure exposure. Revisit extraction when a measured bottleneck, ownership boundary or release conflict persists after simpler fixes. A user-count milestone alone is weak evidence: a million mostly cached page views and a thousand contested stock writes stress different resources.'],
['The decision names an actual constraint.','At least one rejected option has a fair benefit.','The review trigger can be observed.'],['C4'])

chapter('Quality attributes you can measure',
'Turn words such as fast, reliable and scalable into testable scenarios.',
['Functional requirements say what the system does. Quality attributes describe how well it does it under specified conditions. Performance, availability, security, changeability and recoverability often compete. A design cannot maximise them all without cost.',
'Use a scenario: under a stated workload, a named user performs an action, and the system responds within a measurable boundary. Define the measurement point and time window. A server response time is different from the time a shopper waits for payment confirmation.',
'An SLI is an observed indicator. An SLO is a target for that indicator. An SLA is a separate agreement and may have contractual consequences. A recovery time objective describes acceptable restoration time; a recovery point objective describes acceptable data loss measured in time. A backup schedule alone proves neither.'],
['Workload','User action','Measurement','Target'],
'Every target needs a denominator, a window and a clear measurement location.',
'Soko expects an illustrative launch peak of 40 checkout attempts per second. The team proposes that 99% of valid order-submission requests return a durable order reference within two seconds over a rolling 30-day window. Provider confirmation is measured separately: showing pending is acceptable when the provider is slow. A response claiming success before the order is durably stored would make the metric look good while breaking the journey.',
[['Fast acknowledgement','User gets a reference','Final state may remain pending'],['Wait for provider','Immediate final answer sometimes','Long tail and dependency exposure'],['Separate indicators','Failures are easier to locate','More instrumentation to maintain']],
'Averages hide painful tails. Ten very slow checkouts can disappear inside a low average. Report a latency distribution and count failures, including rejected overload where your definition requires it. State planned exclusions explicitly instead of silently removing inconvenient requests.',
'For 100,000 eligible requests and a 99.9% success target, calculate the allowed bad requests. Define one separate indicator for delayed payments and one recovery drill.',
['The request-based allowance is 100 bad requests: 100,000 multiplied by 0.001. This is not a duration-based downtime allowance. If 70 requests have already failed, 30 remain under that simple window calculation.',
'For payments, measure the age distribution of pending attempts and the fraction resolved within the chosen business deadline. For recovery, restore a backup into an isolated environment, verify order counts and reconcile provider references. Record actual restore duration and the latest recoverable timestamp. Those observations test the recovery objectives.'],
['The target specifies eligible traffic.','Pending payment is distinguished from lost order.','The recovery objective is exercised, not assumed.'],['SRE'])

chapter('Draw systems people can understand',
'Use diagrams to explain boundaries, protocols and ownership.',
['A useful diagram answers a particular question. A context diagram places the system among its users and external dependencies. A container view shows major running applications and data stores. In the C4 vocabulary, a container is a deployable or executable unit, not necessarily a Docker container.',
'A component view looks inside a unit. A dynamic view shows a sequence of interactions. A deployment view shows where units run. Avoid putting every level into one picture: a user, a Python method and a database replica rarely belong at the same level of abstraction.',
'Name each arrow with an action or protocol. State who initiates the interaction and whether the caller waits. Mark external systems and trust boundaries. A simple labelled sketch is more informative than a wall of cloud logos. Keep the diagram beside a decision record so changes have a reason.'],
['Shopper','Storefront','Order app','Payment provider'],
'Context sketch: the provider is outside Soko\'s control. Detailed callback arrows belong in a sequence view.',
'A support engineer asks why a shopper has a pending order. A static box labelled Payments does not answer the question. The team draws a sequence: browser submits; app stores pending; worker sends payment request; provider later posts a callback; app records the result. The callback can arrive before the worker receives its original response. Showing this timing exposes the need for stable attempt identifiers and idempotent state transitions.',
[['Context view','Explains scope','Hides runtime detail'],['Sequence view','Exposes timing and failure','Covers only selected scenarios'],['Deployment view','Shows failure domains','Can become vendor-specific']],
'An arrow marked data tells the reader almost nothing. A direct line from browser to database may accidentally imply that clients hold database credentials. Diagrams should reveal security and ownership assumptions rather than conceal them behind ambiguous symbols.',
'Draw the pending-payment sequence with six steps. Add a lost-response branch and a duplicate-callback branch. Identify the durable state after each step.',
['Store an order and a payment-attempt reference before asking the provider to act. If the request times out, keep the attempt unresolved and reconcile using that reference. A callback or a status query may later establish the result.',
'For a duplicate callback, validate its authenticity and use a unique provider event or attempt identity to recognise work already applied. The final state should not oscillate because an old callback arrives late. Your diagram should name the transition guard and the database transaction that protects it.'],
['All external actors are labelled.','Waiting calls and later callbacks are distinguishable.','A reader can find the durable state after a timeout.'],['C4'])

chapter('The layered monolith',
'Understand a single deployable application without equating it with tangled code.',
['A monolith packages its application capabilities into one deployable unit. It can have many modules, background jobs and database tables. It can also run multiple identical instances behind a load balancer. Monolith does not mean one source file, one machine or an inability to scale.',
'A layered design separates transport, application workflow, domain rules and persistence. A controller parses a request; a use case coordinates work; domain logic protects business rules; a repository stores data. The exact names matter less than the direction of dependencies and the responsibilities they express.',
'The convenience is local calls and potentially one local transaction. The risk is accidental coupling: a controller reaches directly into another feature\'s tables, or shared helpers become a dumping ground. Layering alone does not establish business boundaries. Organise features as well as technical responsibilities.'],
['HTTP handler','Order use case','Domain rules','Repository'],
'Call flow descends through responsibilities; domain rules should not require an HTTP request object.',
'Soko\'s first checkout stores an order and reserves stock in one relational transaction. The HTTP handler validates the incoming shape, then calls place_order. The use case checks the price snapshot, reserves stock and records a pending payment request. It does not keep the database transaction open while waiting on the payment provider. A worker performs that external work after the local commit.',
[['Single transaction','Protects local invariants','Limited to participating store'],['One release','Simple deployment path','Features share release risk'],['Multiple instances','Adds request capacity','Shared database may bottleneck']],
'A slow provider call inside an open transaction can hold locks and connections. More application instances then increase database pressure instead of helping. Separate durable acceptance from remote completion before treating service extraction as the cure.',
'Split this handler into responsibilities: read JSON, check quantity, compute total, reserve stock, write order, call provider, render response. Which work belongs outside the transaction?',
['The handler reads JSON and maps errors to responses. Domain/application code checks positive quantities, computes authoritative totals and coordinates reservation plus order insertion. The repository executes persistence operations under the use case\'s transaction boundary.',
'The provider call runs after commit through a durable job or outbox path. Merely starting an in-memory background task after returning a response can lose work on process restart. The response contains the durable order reference and pending status; it does not promise that the payment has completed.'],
['Business rules can be tested without HTTP.','Remote waiting does not hold the local transaction.','The response reflects committed state.'],['MICRO'])

chapter('The modular monolith',
'Keep one deployment while enforcing boundaries around business capabilities.',
['A modular monolith divides the application into coherent modules with explicit interfaces. Orders owns order state; inventory owns availability and reservations; payments owns payment attempts. Other modules call those interfaces rather than updating private tables or importing internal classes.',
'Boundaries are enforced through code organisation, dependency checks and review. A folder named services does not enforce anything by itself. Document allowed imports and expose small public interfaces. Keep shared code narrow: identifiers and technical utilities are safer candidates than a giant shared business model.',
'Modules can share a database engine and still have distinct table ownership. Cross-module transactions are possible in one process, but record where they exist. That coupling affects later extraction. The purpose is understandable change today, not guaranteeing a free migration tomorrow.'],
['Checkout','Orders API','Inventory API','Payments API'],
'Internal APIs are ordinary calls in this design. The diagram does not imply network services.',
'A developer wants to build a warehouse screen by directly joining and updating orders, stock and payment tables. Reading a reporting projection may be acceptable under a documented contract, but writing payment state from the warehouse module breaks ownership. Soko introduces confirm_dispatch on Orders and reserve on Inventory. Payments exposes payment status and transition operations, not its private persistence model.',
[['Module interfaces','Local, explicit collaboration','Needs design discipline'],['Shared write access','Fast first shortcut','Hidden ownership and migrations'],['Boundary checks','Catches illegal imports','Rules need ongoing maintenance']],
'The distributed monolith starts before distribution: features cannot change without coordinated edits everywhere. Moving those same dependencies behind HTTP adds latency without creating independence. Improve the boundary while calls are still cheap to change.',
'Create an ownership table for orders, stock, reservations and payment_attempts. Name one allowed operation for each and one forbidden cross-module write.',
['Orders owns orders and exposes place_order and cancel_order. Inventory owns stock and reservations and exposes reserve, release and confirm_allocation. Payments owns payment_attempts and exposes initiate and record_provider_result. A warehouse action must not directly mark a payment as settled.',
'An order workflow may coordinate the interfaces, but module internals remain private. Add a check that Orders cannot import Payments\' repository implementation. Review any transaction spanning module tables: it may be a valid current choice, but it is also evidence to consider before extraction.'],
['Every writable table has one owning module.','Public operations use business language.','A boundary violation is detectable in review or tooling.'],['MICRO'])

chapter('Ports, adapters and dependency direction',
'Keep business decisions independent of delivery and storage details.',
['A port is an interface through which application logic communicates with the outside. An adapter implements that interface for a particular technology. A payment gateway port could have a provider adapter for deployment and a deterministic fake for tests. Ports are not TCP ports in this vocabulary.',
'Hexagonal and clean architecture approaches emphasise dependency direction: policy should not be controlled by replaceable infrastructure. You can apply that idea inside a monolith or a service. It does not require a large framework or an interface for every function.',
'Test doubles make business scenarios easy to exercise, but they can lie. A fake gateway that always returns immediately misses timeouts, malformed responses and provider-specific limits. Pair fast domain tests with adapter contract tests and a small number of realistic integration checks.'],
['HTTP adapter','Place-order use case','Gateway port','Provider adapter'],
'The use case knows the port contract. The adapter knows the provider\'s protocol.',
'Soko adds a second payment provider. The application asks a gateway to initiate an attempt using amount, currency and a stable reference. Provider A calls it merchant_reference; provider B uses another field. Adapters translate those details and map responses into a small internal result model: accepted, rejected or uncertain. The use case does not inspect vendor-specific JSON.',
[['Provider port','Separates business policy','Contract must cover uncertainty'],['Fake adapter','Fast deterministic tests','Can miss integration behaviour'],['Real adapter tests','Checks protocol mapping','Needs fixtures or sandbox access']],
'An abstraction that maps every failure to False loses information. A rejected payment can safely be shown as rejected; a timeout may mean the provider accepted the request. Preserve uncertainty so later logic can reconcile rather than create another charge.',
'Design a gateway result with three states and a provider reference. Explain which states permit immediate cancellation and which require reconciliation.',
['Use accepted, rejected and uncertain, with structured reason information and a stable attempt reference. Accepted means the provider accepted the request according to the defined contract; it may still be different from settled. Document that distinction instead of using a vague success flag.',
'A definitive rejection can move the attempt to failed when the provider contract supports that conclusion. An uncertain attempt stays pending and is queried or resolved by an authenticated callback. The application should not create a new attempt merely because the adapter could not receive the response.'],
['The core does not import a provider SDK.','The port preserves meaningful failure states.','Adapter tests cover malformed and delayed responses.'],['HEX'])

chapter('Choosing microservice boundaries',
'Split where independent ownership and change justify distributed operation.',
['Microservices organise capabilities as independently deployable services communicating over explicit contracts. Useful boundaries follow business responsibilities and ownership, not a rule that every database table needs a service. A small codebase can still be badly coupled.',
'Look for cohesive rules and change patterns. Inventory allocation may need different scaling and operational ownership from product descriptions. But splitting orders from order lines usually separates things that must change together. Start with the invariant: which facts must be checked atomically?',
'Independence has a price. Each service needs deployment, telemetry, access control, incident ownership and recovery. Network calls create partial failure. Teams must evolve contracts without forcing every consumer to upgrade at the same moment. Service count is not a maturity score.'],
['Business rules','Ownership','Change patterns','Service boundary'],
'Validate the proposed boundary against real collaboration before creating infrastructure.',
'Two years into the illustrative Soko journey, warehouse allocation has a dedicated team and release schedule. Its workloads include long-running imports and stock reconciliation. Those jobs repeatedly disrupt storefront releases. Inventory now has a plausible extraction case. Payments might remain a module until its own ownership and operational needs justify moving it. There is no obligation to extract every module together.',
[['Extract inventory','Independent team and workload','Reservation becomes remote'],['Keep orders together','Preserves order invariants','One release for order features'],['Service per table','Small code units','Chatty workflows and coupling']],
'If checkout must synchronously call eight services before accepting an order, its latency and failure exposure include the whole chain. A service map that looks clean organisationally may be unreliable operationally. Trace the critical journey before approving the split.',
'Choose whether orders and order lines should be separate services. Then propose one better boundary and specify the new failure your design introduces.',
['Keep orders and order lines together when totals, state transitions and line edits belong to one invariant boundary. Separating them adds coordination without a clear independent capability. Inventory is a stronger candidate when a dedicated team owns reservations and stock rules.',
'The new failure is an unavailable or uncertain reservation response. Define a reservation identity, expiry policy and reconciliation path. Order acceptance may become pending rather than immediately confirmed. That product behaviour must be agreed with support and users, not hidden inside infrastructure work.'],
['The service has a named operational owner.','Independent deployment is meaningful.','Cross-boundary invariants have an explicit strategy.'],['MICRO'])

chapter('Synchronous APIs and contracts',
'Design requests that remain understandable when networks are slow or unreliable.',
['Synchronous communication means the caller waits for a response in the interaction being considered. HTTP and gRPC can both support request-response APIs. Choose according to clients, tooling, contract needs and operating experience rather than assuming one protocol creates better architecture.',
'A contract includes more than fields: status semantics, timeouts, authentication, pagination, compatibility and retry behaviour matter. Returning 202 Accepted acknowledges processing under the stated contract; it does not mean the business action has finished. Provide a reference or status endpoint for pending work.',
'Bound latency with a total deadline and per-dependency budgets. Connection and read timeouts may cover different phases; a read timeout alone is not necessarily an end-to-end limit. Propagate cancellation where supported, but do not assume cancellation reverses a remote side effect.'],
['Client deadline','Gateway','Order API','Dependency budget'],
'Time spent earlier in the request reduces the budget available to later calls.',
'Soko\'s app submits POST /orders with a client-generated idempotency key. The server stores the accepted order and returns an order URL. If the mobile network drops the response, the app retries the same logical request with the same key. A later GET returns the current state. A different basket using the same key is rejected as a conflict instead of silently returning an unrelated order.',
[['Wait for completion','Simple success path','Long waits and uncertain outcomes'],['Accept then poll','Short durable acknowledgement','Client must handle pending'],['Webhook update','Less polling','Authentication and retry contract']],
'Renaming a response field can break deployed mobile apps that cannot upgrade immediately. Even adding an enum value can break exhaustive client logic. Compatibility tests should represent real consumers, not merely prove that the new server accepts its own requests.',
'Write responses for a newly accepted order, a repeated identical request and a repeated key with a different payload. State what the client does after a timeout.',
['A new request returns a durable order identity and pending state. The identical retry returns the same logical outcome under the idempotency contract; its status code may follow the documented replay policy. A changed payload with the same key returns a clear conflict.',
'After a timeout, reuse the key or query by the stored reference. Do not interpret silence as rejection. Keep the key scoped to the caller and operation, store a request fingerprint, and define retention. Expiring the record too early can permit a late duplicate.'],
['Timeout behaviour is documented.','Retries preserve logical request identity.','Compatibility includes errors and enum values.'],['HTTP','RETRY'])

chapter('Data ownership and consistency',
'Locate the source of truth and protect invariants under concurrent work.',
['A source of truth is the authority for a particular fact, not necessarily one database for the whole company. Inventory may own available stock while Orders owns the customer\'s order state. Copies for search or reporting do not gain authority just because they are convenient to query.',
'Local transactions can protect multiple changes within a database. Distributed workflows require additional coordination or a deliberate relaxation of when all views agree. Eventual consistency means replicas or derived views can converge under stated conditions; it is not a promise that every failure fixes itself.',
'The CAP tradeoff concerns consistency and availability during a network partition under specific definitions. It is not a general instruction to choose any two of three labels for an entire application. Different operations can make different choices. A stale product description may be acceptable while a stock reservation requires an authoritative check.'],
['Authoritative write','Committed state','Async projection','Reader view'],
'The reader view can lag. Define when the application must return to the authoritative owner.',
'One unit of a popular router remains in Soko\'s warehouse. Two shoppers read a cached availability of one. Both attempt checkout. A read-then-write sequence without concurrency control can oversell. Inventory instead performs a conditional update that decrements stock only if enough remains, inside the reservation transaction. Exactly one of the competing reservations can succeed under the database\'s enforced condition.',
[['Authoritative reservation','Protects stock invariant','Adds latency and contention'],['Cached availability','Fast browsing','Cannot authorise a reservation'],['Async read model','Efficient queries','Needs lag and repair handling']],
'Giving every service write access to the same tables creates hidden coupling even if each runs in a separate container. Database-per-service means logical ownership and access boundaries; it does not necessarily require a separate physical database server for every service.',
'For a ticket-booking system, identify which reads may be stale and which write must be exclusive. Describe the outcome when the seat authority cannot be reached.',
['A venue description, image or approximate availability count may be stale within an agreed tolerance. Confirming ownership of seat A12 must be protected by the booking authority using a constraint, conditional update or appropriate locking strategy.',
'If that authority is unreachable, return pending or unavailable rather than inventing confirmation from a cache. This sacrifices immediate completion to protect the seat invariant. A later reconciliation process must resolve pending attempts and release expired holds according to a documented policy.'],
['Each important fact has an owner.','Staleness tolerance is specified per operation.','Concurrent writes cannot bypass the invariant.'],['CAP','SQL'])

chapter('Events, commands and queues',
'Choose asynchronous messages for a specific coordination problem.',
['A command asks a responsible receiver to do something: ReserveStock. An event reports a fact that has happened: StockReserved. Both can travel through messaging infrastructure, but their meanings and ownership differ. Name events in the past tense and include enough context to interpret them.',
'A work queue distributes tasks among workers. Publish-subscribe lets independent subscribers react to the same event. A retained event log supports consumers reading positions over time. Products can support more than one model, so compare required semantics rather than relying only on a product category.',
'Asynchronous processing decouples timing, not every dependency. Consumers still depend on contracts, identifiers and business meaning. Plan for duplicate messages, delayed processing, invalid payloads and recovery. A message accepted by infrastructure is not proof that every business effect has completed.'],
['Order committed','OrderPlaced','Notification worker','Analytics consumer'],
'Notifications and analytics are independent reactions; a slow report need not block checkout.',
'Soko wants confirmation emails and daily sales analysis. Neither should delay order acceptance. Orders publishes OrderPlaced after the order is committed. Notifications turns that fact into a delivery task; analytics updates a report. Inventory reservation is a different requirement: if checkout needs an immediate reservation decision, merely broadcasting an event does not make the invariant disappear.',
[['Command queue','Clear work ownership','Receiver and retry policy required'],['Event subscription','Independent reactions','Contracts and lag still couple systems'],['Direct API','Immediate decision','Caller waits on receiver health']],
'An event called ProcessEverything is really an ambiguous command. Consumers guess what must happen, and no owner knows when the workflow is complete. Separate facts from requests and document who is responsible for each state transition.',
'Classify SendReceipt, PaymentSettled and RebuildSearchIndex. For each, name the owner and decide whether multiple independent subscribers make sense.',
['SendReceipt is a command owned by the notification capability. PaymentSettled is a fact owned by Payments and may interest Orders, accounting and analytics. RebuildSearchIndex is an operational command owned by search; broadcasting it as a business fact would be misleading.',
'An email worker may have multiple competing instances while accounting and Orders each independently receive PaymentSettled. Those are different subscription relationships. Give each logical consumer its own progress tracking, failure policy and replay rules.'],
['Messages distinguish requests from facts.','Each required effect has an accountable owner.','Queue acceptance is not labelled business completion.'],['KINTRO'])

chapter('Kafka: topics, partitions and offsets',
'Understand Kafka as a retained event log rather than a magic service connector.',
['Kafka stores records in topics divided into partitions. Each partition is an ordered log, and records have positions called offsets. There is no single total order across all partitions in a topic. An offset is a position within one partition, not a global business identifier.',
'A producer can use a key to route related records consistently when its partitioning strategy and partition count remain suitable. Choosing order_id can preserve per-order locality; choosing customer_id creates a different ordering boundary. Increasing partition count can change key placement, so migration and ordering assumptions need review.',
'Retention determines how long records remain available; consumption does not inherently delete them. Replication supports durability and availability under configured conditions. Log compaction is a separate retention mechanism centred on keys; it does not mean every historical update is preserved forever.'],
['Producer + key','Topic partitions','Stored offsets','Independent readers'],
'Ordering is per partition. The key choice should match the entity whose sequence matters.',
'Soko publishes order lifecycle events using order_id. Order O-42 receives OrderPlaced, PaymentSettled and DispatchConfirmed. A consumer can reason about that order\'s sequence when production, partitioning and processing preserve it. Events for O-43 may be in another partition and processed first. The daily report must not infer global chronology from offsets across partitions.',
[['Key by order','Per-order locality','A hot order can concentrate work'],['More partitions','More potential parallelism','Ordering and operations need review'],['Longer retention','More replay history','Higher storage and privacy burden']],
'A producer acknowledgement is governed by producer and broker configuration; it is not a universal guarantee against all loss. Set replication, acknowledgements and in-sync replica policies together. A single local broker demonstrates APIs but provides no multi-broker resilience.',
'Given partition 0 offsets 7 and 8 and partition 1 offset 2, identify which ordering claims are valid. Choose a key for delivery-driver location updates and explain the tradeoff.',
['Within partition 0, offset 7 precedes offset 8 in the log. Offset 2 in partition 1 cannot be compared as a global position. A timestamp is useful context but does not automatically create trustworthy global ordering across producers.',
'Driver_id is a plausible key when the latest trajectory of each driver matters. It keeps one driver\'s events together under a stable partition strategy, but a very active driver can create uneven load. For an analytical workload that does not need per-driver sequence, a different distribution may be acceptable.'],
['The ordering claim names a partition or entity.','Retention and replay needs are explicit.','Key choice is reviewed before changing partition count.'],['KDESIGN','KINTRO'])

chapter('Kafka consumers, groups and replay',
'Scale consumption while keeping progress and recovery understandable.',
['In a conventional Kafka consumer group, partitions are assigned among group members; one member at a time owns a partition assignment within that group. Separate groups can independently read the same topic. This chapter covers consumer groups, not the distinct share-group consumption model.',
'The committed offset records where a group should resume, conventionally the next record to read. Processing progress and committed progress can differ. Rebalances move assignments as membership changes, so consumers need careful shutdown, polling and offset handling.',
'Replay is powerful only when side effects are controlled. Rebuilding a search index can be safe in an isolated destination. Replaying an email consumer may resend old receipts. Record lag and event age, but remember that an offset gap measures records, not elapsed time or business impact.'],
['Orders topic','Group assignments','Process records','Commit progress'],
'A different consumer group has its own progress. Adding workers does not create more partitions.',
'Soko\'s orders topic has six partitions. Its search group has three consumers, which can share those assignments. Growing the group to eight conventional consumers leaves at least two without a partition assignment at that moment. A separate analytics group reads all partitions independently. The search team rebuilds a new index under a new group identity, compares results, then switches readers.',
[['One group, many members','Distributes work','Parallelism bounded by partitions'],['Separate groups','Independent reactions','Each adds processing and lag'],['Replay to new destination','Safe comparison and cutover','Needs retained history and capacity']],
'Committing before durable processing can skip work after a crash. Processing first and crashing before commit can repeat work. A worker that launches untracked parallel tasks can also commit past unfinished records. Completion and offset advancement must be coordinated per partition.',
'A worker processes offsets 10, 11 and 12 concurrently. Only 10 and 12 finish. What is the safe next-offset commit boundary? What would committing 13 risk?',
['The safe contiguous boundary is 11, meaning records below 11 are complete and offset 11 remains to be read. Finishing 12 does not make the gap at 11 disappear. A restart from 11 may repeat 12, so effects need idempotency.',
'Committing 13 would tell the group to resume after all three records. If the process then dies, unfinished work at 11 may be skipped. Prefer simple sequential processing per partition until the throughput need justifies the complexity of tracking concurrent completions.'],
['Committed progress cannot pass unfinished work.','Replay side effects have been reviewed.','Consumer count is compared with partition count.'],['KDESIGN','KCONSUMER'])

chapter('Delivery guarantees and idempotency',
'Protect business effects when the same request or event appears again.',
['At-most-once processing can lose work when progress is recorded before the effect. At-least-once processing permits repetition when an effect succeeds but progress is not recorded. These labels describe a defined boundary; they do not automatically describe the outcome in every external system.',
'Idempotency means repeating a logical operation does not add another unintended effect. A unique operation identity and a durable record of its outcome are common ingredients. The record and the business update must be coordinated; storing a processed flag separately can leave another crash gap.',
'Kafka producer idempotence and transactions address particular Kafka operations. Transactional read-process-write can coordinate Kafka output and consumer offsets when configured correctly. An email, bank API or unrelated database is not automatically part of that transaction. Define the end-to-end boundary before saying exactly once.'],
['Receive event','Check identity','Commit effect','Advance progress'],
'The identity check and durable effect belong together. Progress is acknowledged after that commit.',
'Soko receives PaymentSettled twice because the publisher retried after losing an acknowledgement. Orders must not dispatch two packages. It records the event identity and the order transition in one database transaction. A repeated identity becomes a no-op. Separately, a dispatch operation has its own stable identity because two distinct events can still refer to the same logical fulfilment action.',
[['Deduplicate event ID','Handles transport repeats','Does not detect all semantic duplicates'],['Business uniqueness','Protects logical effect','Needs carefully chosen scope'],['Kafka transaction','Coordinates Kafka work','Does not cover arbitrary external effects']],
'A set of processed IDs in process memory disappears on restart. A durable set without an atomic effect update is also incomplete. For external side effects, use the provider\'s idempotency contract where available and reconcile uncertain results instead of pretending a local flag proves completion.',
'An event changes an order, then the process crashes before acknowledging it. Explain what the next delivery should do. What changes if the effect is an external email send?',
['If the order update and processed identity committed together, redelivery finds the identity and leaves the order unchanged. If the transaction rolled back, redelivery can apply it normally. The companion SQLite lab exercises both cases.',
'Email sending cannot usually be committed atomically with the local database. Use a durable send task and a provider idempotency facility if offered. Otherwise acknowledge the residual duplicate risk and build delivery tracking. A local database transaction alone cannot guarantee one physical email at the recipient.'],
['Identity survives process restart.','The deduplication record and local effect are atomic.','The guarantee names the systems it covers.'],['KDESIGN','IDEMP'])

chapter('The transactional outbox',
'Keep a database change and its publication intent together.',
['The dual-write problem appears when code updates a database and separately publishes a message. Either action can succeed while the other fails. Reversing their order simply changes which inconsistent state is possible.',
'An outbox stores the business change and a publication record in the same local transaction. A relay later publishes committed outbox records. This makes publication retryable from durable state. The relay may publish more than once if it crashes after publishing but before recording completion.',
'An outbox therefore does not remove the need for idempotent consumers. Decide how records are claimed, ordered, retried, monitored and eventually removed. A polling relay and change-data-capture relay have different operational costs. Start with the simplest mechanism that meets the measured workload.'],
['Local transaction','Order + outbox','Relay','Event stream'],
'The first two boxes describe one atomic write boundary; the relay is an independent retrying process.',
'Soko commits order O-42 and event E-42 in one transaction. The relay publishes E-42, but loses its connection before it can mark the row sent. On restart it publishes E-42 again. The order is not missing and the publication intent was not lost; the consumer handles the repeated event identity. A dashboard tracks the age of the oldest unpublished row.',
[['Database then publish','Easy to write','Can lose publication intent'],['Outbox + polling','Durable, inspectable backlog','Polling and cleanup work'],['Outbox + CDC','Streams committed changes','Connector and schema operations']],
'A relay that marks a row sent before publishing can lose the event. A relay that never expires or archives completed rows can fill the database. Monitor both pending age and total growth. For multiple relays, use a deliberate claiming protocol rather than letting every worker race over the same rows.',
'Run the outbox companion lab. Inject a failure after publication but before marking sent. Predict the number of message deliveries and the final order count.',
['The simulated broker receives the same event twice after the retry. The consumer\'s transactional identity check applies the order effect once. The publisher still records the row as sent only after its publish call returns successfully.',
'The lab models the crash window using SQLite and an in-memory transport; it is not a Kafka broker or a production relay. Its purpose is to make the failure ordering visible. A deployed relay also needs concurrent claims, durable transport acknowledgements, retry limits and operational recovery.'],
['Business state and publication intent commit together.','The relay tolerates repeated publication.','Old pending rows trigger investigation.'],['OUTBOX'])

chapter('Sagas and business compensation',
'Coordinate a long workflow without pretending it is one local transaction.',
['A saga is a sequence of local steps with a policy for failure and compensation. Each service commits its own work. Compensation is a new business action, such as releasing a reservation or initiating a refund; it is not a time machine that erases every consequence.',
'Orchestration puts workflow coordination in an explicit component. Choreography lets participants react to events. Either can work, but visibility, ownership and complexity differ. A workflow with many timeouts and exceptional paths often benefits from an explicit durable state machine.',
'Sagas do not automatically provide isolation. Another workflow may observe intermediate state. Use reservations, version checks or other business rules to protect conflicting operations. Compensation itself may fail, so persist its state and provide retry and manual recovery paths.'],
['Reserve stock','Request payment','Confirm order','Release or reconcile'],
'Success, rejection and uncertainty take different branches. Unknown payment outcomes need reconciliation.',
'Soko reserves one router, starts payment and waits. A definitive payment rejection releases the reservation. A timeout is different: the provider might have accepted payment, so the saga records payment_unknown. If a late settlement arrives after a reservation has expired, the business must choose re-reservation, substitution or refund. That decision cannot be delegated to Kafka.',
[['Orchestration','Visible workflow state','Coordinator must be reliable'],['Choreography','Local reactions','End-to-end flow harder to follow'],['Local transaction','Strong local boundary','Cannot include every remote effect']],
'Automatically refunding on every timeout can refund an unknown charge, race with late callbacks or create inconsistent support messages. Write transitions for definitive rejection, uncertainty, late success and failed compensation separately. Include an operator-visible terminal exception state.',
'Design states for reserved, payment_unknown, confirmed, release_pending and cancelled. Which event permits confirmed? What happens if release fails?',
['A verified settlement plus a valid reservation permits confirmed under this example\'s policy. A timeout moves to payment_unknown, not cancelled. A definitive rejection requests release and enters release_pending. Successful release allows cancelled.',
'If release fails, retain release_pending and retry the same logical release identity. Escalate when age exceeds the operational threshold. The companion state-machine lab checks that uncertain payment does not release stock and that repeated confirmations do not create a second transition.'],
['Every uncertain outcome has a durable state.','Compensations can be retried safely.','Support can locate workflows needing intervention.'],['SAGA'])

chapter('CQRS, projections and event sourcing',
'Separate three ideas that are often incorrectly treated as one package.',
['CQRS separates command-side responsibilities from query-side responsibilities. It can begin with different models in one application and one database. It does not require Kafka, microservices or event sourcing.',
'A projection is a derived view built for a query: orders by day, available products or a customer timeline. It may lag behind authoritative writes. Define its rebuild source and how readers handle that lag. A projection that cannot be repaired becomes another fragile source of truth.',
'Event sourcing stores the authoritative history of domain changes as events and derives current state from that history. Publishing events from an ordinary CRUD database is not automatically event sourcing. Event sourcing adds demands around event evolution, replay, snapshots and deletion policies; adopt it for a clear domain need.'],
['Command model','Authoritative state','Projection update','Query model'],
'This diagram shows CQRS with a projection. The authoritative state need not be an event store.',
'Soko needs a dashboard of daily settled totals. Orders and Payments remain ordinary database-backed modules. A reporting projection consumes settlement facts and builds a daily summary. The UI displays the last processed timestamp. Rebuilding into a new table and comparing totals is safer than overwriting the only report in place. Financial reconciliation still checks provider records and authoritative payment state.',
[['CRUD + queries','Simple operating model','Complex reports can burden writes'],['Separate projection','Query-specific performance','Lag and rebuild work'],['Event sourcing','Rich domain history','Long-lived event contracts']],
'A replay that blindly sums duplicate settlement events inflates revenue. A snapshot without a clear event position can also produce double counting or omissions. Use stable identities, version rules and reconciliation totals; test a rebuild against a known small history.',
'For a school portal, decide whether a student timetable needs full event sourcing. Design a projection freshness indicator and a rebuild check.',
['A timetable usually needs a current schedule and perhaps an audit trail; those requirements alone do not require full event sourcing. A normal authoritative schedule database with a derived student view can be sufficient.',
'Show last refreshed time and, where useful, a version identifier. Rebuild a candidate view from a controlled snapshot plus subsequent changes under a consistent boundary. Compare student/course assignments and conflict counts before switching readers. A stale or incomplete view should be visible, not silently treated as authoritative.'],
['CQRS is distinguished from event sourcing.','The projection has a reproducible rebuild path.','Duplicate and late events have defined handling.'],['CQRS','ES'])

chapter('Timeouts, retries and overload',
'Prevent a small dependency failure from consuming the whole system.',
['A timeout bounds waiting; a retry repeats an attempt; backoff spaces attempts; jitter spreads them. These mechanisms solve different problems. Retrying validation failures usually wastes work, while retrying an uncertain side effect without idempotency may duplicate it.',
'A circuit breaker can stop repeated calls to an unhealthy dependency and later allow probes. A bulkhead limits how much capacity one dependency or workload can consume. Backpressure signals that producers must slow down. A queue absorbs bursts only within its capacity and acceptable delay; it cannot create processing capacity.',
'Retry budgets and deadlines should apply across a user journey. If every layer retries independently, attempts multiply. Decide which layer owns retries and expose the final outcome clearly. Fallbacks must preserve business meaning: cached product text may be fine, fabricated payment success is not.'],
['Deadline','Bounded attempts','Backoff + jitter','Fail or defer'],
'Every repeated attempt consumes time and capacity. Stop when either budget is exhausted.',
'A provider slowdown causes Soko checkout workers to occupy all connections. The team caps concurrent provider calls, separates receipt workers from checkout workers, and uses a bounded retry policy for transient failures. Pending payment attempts remain durable for reconciliation. The storefront can still serve catalog pages while the payment path degrades.',
[['Unlimited retries','May eventually succeed','Can amplify overload indefinitely'],['Bounded retries','Limits extra work','Some requests need later recovery'],['Queue + backpressure','Absorbs temporary bursts','Adds delay and capacity limits']],
'Assume two layers each allow three total attempts. One user action can drive nine downstream calls, not six. If requests already exceed capacity, extra attempts worsen the queue. Measure retry volume separately from original traffic.',
'A worker completes 80 tasks per second while arrivals remain at 100 per second for five minutes. Estimate backlog growth. Then choose one action that reduces demand and one that increases effective capacity.',
['The backlog grows by 20 tasks per second for 300 seconds: 6,000 tasks, ignoring initial backlog and variability. A queue length limit without an overload response only changes where failure occurs.',
'Demand can be reduced by rejecting or deferring nonessential work and limiting retries. Capacity can improve through a measured bottleneck fix or additional independent workers when the downstream system can sustain them. Adding workers against a saturated database can reduce effective capacity instead.'],
['Retries are bounded and owned by one layer.','Side-effect retries preserve identity.','Overload has an explicit user-visible behaviour.'],['RETRY','CIRCUIT'])

chapter('Caching, capacity and scaling',
'Optimise the resource that measurements identify as limiting useful throughput.',
['Vertical scaling gives a unit more resources. Horizontal scaling adds units. Neither removes a serial bottleneck or a shared hot record. First identify whether CPU, storage latency, locks, connection limits or an external service constrains the journey.',
'A cache stores a reusable result closer to the reader. Define keys, expiry, invalidation and acceptable staleness. Include tenant and authorisation scope where relevant. Cache-aside reads the cache first and loads from the source on a miss; simultaneous misses can create a stampede.',
'Partitioning distributes data or work across units. Replication creates copies for availability or reads. These are different tools. Both introduce coordination and recovery work. Capacity estimates should state units and assumptions, then be checked with representative workloads and headroom.'],
['Measure demand','Find bottleneck','Change one thing','Compare outcome'],
'Scale from evidence. Include cost and tail latency in the comparison, not only average throughput.',
'Soko\'s catalog has 95% cache hits under an illustrative test at 1,000 requests per second. About 50 requests per second reach the source if each miss causes one source read. A full expiry can briefly send far more traffic to the database. The team adds staggered expiry, request coalescing and a stale-read policy for descriptions. Stock reservation still checks the authority.',
[['Cache descriptions','Lower read load','Stale content and invalidation'],['Read replica','Moves some queries','Replication lag and routing'],['More app instances','More request concurrency','May overload shared dependencies']],
'A global cache key such as dashboard can leak one tenant\'s data to another. A fast response that violates authorisation is not a performance improvement. Key design and access checks belong in the architecture review, not only in an implementation detail.',
'Calculate source reads at 80% and 99% hit rates for the same 1,000 requests per second. Name one reason the simple estimate may understate database load.',
['At 80% hits, 200 requests per second miss; at 99%, 10 miss. These are request counts, not necessarily SQL query counts. A miss that performs five queries multiplies the database work.',
'Retries, stampedes, background refresh and write traffic also add load. A benchmark should include realistic hit distribution, hot keys and failure recovery. Measure cache warm-up separately from steady state and verify that stale data never authorises a protected write.'],
['Units and workload assumptions are stated.','Cache keys include the relevant security scope.','The benchmark includes cold or expired cache behaviour.'],['CACHE'])

chapter('Security and tenant isolation',
'Make trust boundaries and data access part of the structure of the system.',
['Authentication establishes an identity; authorisation decides what that identity may do. Encryption protects data in transit or storage under its threat model, but it does not decide whether a user may read another customer\'s order. Each service must enforce the access rules relevant to its resources.',
'Use least-privilege service identities and narrow credentials. A gateway can centralise some checks, but internal services should not blindly trust caller-supplied tenant headers. Derive security context from validated identity and preserve it through requests and messages.',
'Events are durable data distribution. Publishing addresses, tokens or unnecessary personal details spreads the security and retention burden to every consumer and backup. Minimise payloads, classify topics and restrict producer/consumer permissions. Design credential rotation and incident response before an emergency.'],
['Validated identity','Tenant context','Resource policy','Audited operation'],
'Authorisation is evaluated for the resource and action, not merely for successful login.',
'Soko later serves multiple merchants. Merchant A requests order 500, which belongs to merchant B. The order API checks both the requested ID and the authenticated merchant scope. The cache key includes that scope. A warehouse event contains only the fields required for fulfilment; analytics receives a narrower representation. Internal network location alone does not grant access.',
[['Gateway checks','Consistent entry policy','Cannot replace resource checks'],['Service authorisation','Protects owned resources','Requires shared identity contracts'],['Minimal events','Reduces exposure','Consumers may need authorised lookups']],
'Filtering only the list endpoint while leaving GET /orders/{id} unscoped leaks data. So can exporting reports through a less-protected worker. Include object-level access tests across HTTP, jobs, files and caches.',
'Design three tests for tenant isolation: direct lookup, cached response and asynchronous export. Explain what must happen when the tenant context is absent.',
['Each test creates data for A and B, authenticates as A and attempts to access B\'s result. Direct lookup must not return the record; cache reuse must remain scoped; export generation and download must enforce the same ownership rules.',
'An absent or invalid tenant context should fail closed for tenant-protected operations. Do not substitute an all-tenants default. Logs can record a safe request identifier and denial reason without exposing the protected record or credentials.'],
['Tenant context comes from validated identity.','Object-level authorisation covers all delivery paths.','Messages contain only necessary data.'],['OWASP'])

chapter('Observability and operations',
'Make it possible to explain a user-visible failure across boundaries.',
['Metrics summarise behaviour over time; logs record discrete details; traces connect operations across a request or workflow. They complement each other. A trace does not replace business reconciliation, and a dashboard of CPU graphs does not explain why a customer has no order confirmation.',
'Propagate correlation identifiers across requests and events, while avoiding secrets in telemetry. Use bounded labels in metrics: putting every order ID into a metric label creates high cardinality. Put detailed identifiers in appropriately controlled logs or traces instead.',
'Alert on meaningful symptoms and sustained risk. Queue age, failed transitions and payment uncertainty may matter more than raw queue size. Every alert should have an owner and an initial action. A runbook should state how to diagnose, contain and recover, including how to verify that recovery worked.'],
['User symptom','Metric signal','Trace + logs','Recovery action'],
'Start with the affected journey, then use technical evidence to locate the cause.',
'Soko sees normal web response times but an increase in orders pending for more than ten minutes. A workflow-age metric alerts the on-call engineer. Correlation IDs connect the order, payment attempt and provider response. The cause is an expired provider credential used by the worker. Rotating it restores new work; the team then reconciles the existing pending attempts rather than assuming the backlog fixed itself.',
[['Infrastructure metrics','Show resource health','May miss business failure'],['Workflow metrics','Show customer impact','Need domain-specific definitions'],['Distributed traces','Connect dependency calls','Sampling and storage cost']],
'Logging every request body can expose credentials and personal data while creating expensive noise. Redact sensitive fields, control retention and test redaction. Observability should help operators without creating another data leak.',
'Write an alert and a five-step runbook for growing outbox age. Include a step that distinguishes a blocked publisher from a slow consumer.',
['Alert when the oldest unsent outbox record exceeds the agreed threshold for a sustained interval. First confirm impact and current age; then inspect relay health and publish errors; check broker reachability and permissions; apply the documented repair; finally verify that unsent age falls and downstream effects reconcile.',
'A healthy relay with growing consumer lag is a different incident: publication may be succeeding while processing is stalled. Compare outbox progress, broker acknowledgements and consumer event age before changing the wrong component.'],
['Alerts name a user or workflow impact.','Correlation survives asynchronous boundaries.','Recovery includes a correctness check.'],['OTEL','SRE'])

chapter('Testing an architecture',
'Test contracts and failure boundaries, not only individual functions.',
['Unit tests check local rules quickly. Integration tests exercise real adapters and stores. Contract tests check what consumers expect from an interface. End-to-end tests cover selected journeys through the assembled system. These layers answer different questions; one cannot replace all the others.',
'Architecture tests can detect forbidden imports, missing ownership rules or undocumented public endpoints. Failure tests explore crashes, timeouts, duplicates and delayed messages. Make the injection point explicit: before commit, after commit, after publication or before acknowledgement produce different outcomes.',
'A passing local simulation proves only its model. Real infrastructure adds configuration, permissions, concurrency and network behaviour. Keep a small representative integration suite and record environment versions. Test restores and rollback paths as part of release readiness, not only after a production incident.'],
['Rule tests','Adapter tests','Contract tests','Journey + failure tests'],
'Test depth grows across boundaries. Use each level for a distinct kind of evidence.',
'Soko\'s checkout unit tests pass, but a schema change removes currency from a payment message. The local publisher and its tests changed together, so they remain green. A consumer contract fixture from the payment service catches the missing field. Another test replays the same settlement event and verifies that the dispatch count remains one.',
[['Unit tests','Fast rule feedback','Cannot validate broker configuration'],['Contract tests','Protect independent consumers','Need representative contracts'],['Failure injection','Exercises recovery assumptions','Must control scope and cleanup']],
'An end-to-end happy-path test can pass while every recovery path is broken. Conversely, thousands of unit tests can coexist with a misconfigured network policy. Map each major architectural claim to the type of test that can actually falsify it.',
'Create a test matrix for order creation and event publication with crashes before commit, after commit and after publish. State the invariant for every row.',
['Before commit, neither order nor outbox intent should persist. After commit, both must exist even if the relay has not run. After publish but before marking sent, retry may produce another delivery, but the downstream business effect must remain singular.',
'The companion tests exercise these boundaries with SQLite and a simulated transport. Extend the matrix in a real deployment with broker outage, consumer restart, database restore and multiple relay workers. Treat those as additional evidence, not as results already established by the local lab.'],
['Each guarantee has an appropriate test boundary.','Consumer contracts are represented independently.','Failure tests check final state, not only exceptions.'],['OUTBOX','KDESIGN'])

chapter('Deployment, containers and serverless',
'Choose an operating model and release strategy that the team can support.',
['A container packages an application with a defined runtime environment. An orchestrator schedules and manages running units. Serverless platforms manage more of the execution infrastructure for you. None of these choices determines whether your application is a monolith or microservices.',
'Choose according to workload duration, scaling pattern, state, networking, cost and operating experience. A managed application platform may be enough for a small team. Kubernetes can offer useful controls, but those controls also require expertise. Serverless execution can simplify bursty tasks while introducing limits and lifecycle assumptions to understand.',
'Releases change code and often data. Use backward-compatible migrations where possible: expand the schema, deploy compatible code, migrate data, then remove obsolete fields after old readers are gone. Rolling back a binary does not automatically undo a destructive database migration.'],
['Compatible schema','New code','Verify behaviour','Retire old shape'],
'Expand and contract separates compatibility changes from irreversible cleanup.',
'Soko renames a payment status field. Instead of deleting the old column immediately, the team adds the new representation, supports the transition, backfills data and verifies counts. Only after all readers have moved does it remove the old field. A small canary release compares error rates and pending-age behaviour before broader rollout.',
[['Managed app platform','Lower operational setup','Platform constraints'],['Containers + orchestration','Flexible runtime control','Cluster and policy operations'],['Serverless functions','Useful for bursty tasks','Execution and state constraints']],
'A readiness check that says process alive may send traffic to an application that cannot serve requests. A liveness check tied to a brief database outage can cause a restart storm. Define startup, readiness and liveness according to what each signal controls.',
'Plan a release that adds an optional delivery_note field used by one service and ignored by older consumers. Include a rollback and a data verification step.',
['Add the nullable or otherwise compatible field first, then deploy producers that may send it and consumers that tolerate its absence. Verify old consumers also tolerate unknown fields under their actual schema rules. Compare record counts and representative payloads during the canary.',
'Rollback restores the prior application version while leaving the compatible schema in place. Do not drop the field as part of an emergency rollback. Remove transitional code only after consumers and retained events have been considered. Compatibility includes data still waiting in queues.'],
['The platform choice names an operational reason.','Old and new versions can coexist during rollout.','Rollback accounts for persisted data and queued events.'],['K8S','SERVERLESS'])

chapter('Migration without a big-bang rewrite',
'Extract one capability with a controlled cutover and a measurable exit criterion.',
['A strangler migration gradually routes selected behaviour to a new implementation while the old system continues serving the rest. Start with a capability whose boundary is understood. Separate refactoring the boundary from changing its deployment whenever possible.',
'Data migration needs a source-of-truth policy. Backfills, ongoing changes and cutover can race. Record a consistent starting point and a way to capture later updates. Shadow reads can compare results without serving them, but shadow writes with real side effects are dangerous.',
'Define success and rollback before traffic moves. A migration is not complete when the new service answers a demo request. It is complete when ownership, monitoring, recovery, data correctness and operational responsibility are established, and the old path can be retired safely.'],
['Stabilise boundary','Backfill + compare','Route gradually','Retire old path'],
'Each stage has an exit check. Reversibility decreases after data ownership changes.',
'Soko extracts catalog search first because it is read-heavy and has a rebuildable index. The monolith remains the product authority. The new search service builds an index from a snapshot and subsequent changes, compares result samples, then serves a small traffic share. Checkout still validates price and stock with their owners. Search becoming unavailable must not corrupt orders.',
[['Read-only extraction','Lower side-effect risk','Does not prove write migration readiness'],['Gradual traffic shift','Limits exposure','Requires routing and comparison'],['Big-bang replacement','One visible cutover','Large rollback and discovery risk']],
'Dual writing to old and new databases without a reconciliation plan creates two competing truths. During a rollback, deciding which one contains the valid recent writes becomes a business problem. Prefer one authoritative write path and explicit propagation while the migration is in progress.',
'Create a cutover checklist for search. Then explain why the same checklist is insufficient for moving stock reservations.',
['Search needs index completeness, query quality checks, freshness bounds, capacity evidence, routing rollback and an owner for repair. If it fails, traffic can return to the old search path while products remain authoritative elsewhere.',
'Stock reservations also require exclusive write ownership, active-reservation transfer, concurrency guarantees and a policy for in-flight requests. Rolling traffic back while both systems can reserve the same units risks overselling. The migration must coordinate the authority transition, not merely copy rows.'],
['One authority remains clear during the transition.','Cutover includes in-flight work.','The old path has a defined retirement condition.'],['STRANGLER'])

chapter('Capstone: choose the architecture',
'Defend a complete design using workload, risk, ownership and recovery evidence.',
['Architecture review is a comparison of alternatives under constraints. Explain the user journey, invariants, quality targets, data ownership and deployment approach before listing technologies. A good review names what the system will do when a dependency is unavailable.',
'Use cost as an engineering input. Count developer effort, operations, managed-service charges, storage, data transfer and incident recovery. A system that is inexpensive per request but requires constant specialist intervention may be a poor fit for a small organisation.',
'Your final design should contain a context diagram, runtime view, one failure sequence, an ownership table and a decision record. Include a staged evolution path. Separate required mechanisms from optional future improvements so the first release remains achievable.'],
['Constraints','Candidate designs','Failure review','Decision + review date'],
'The deliverable is an argued decision with evidence, not the largest possible box diagram.',
'Compare three fictional workloads. A school portal has one small team, predictable enrolment peaks and sensitive student records. A ticketing system has short bursts and strict seat uniqueness. A logistics platform receives ongoing location events from many devices and serves independent operational and analytical consumers. All three need reliable software, but their dominant problems differ.',
[['School portal','Modular app + jobs fits early needs','Protect tenant data and backups'],['Ticket booking','Authoritative reservation boundary','Control contention and hold expiry'],['Logistics stream','Partitioned ingestion may fit','Plan keys, lag, retention and cost']],
'Using Kafka for a school portal solely because it appears in a modern stack adds operational work without proving value. Conversely, forcing every logistics consumer into one synchronous ingestion request couples device acceptance to unrelated reporting. Match the mechanism to the requirement.',
'Design Soko\'s next release for a five-person team, two warehouses and delayed payment callbacks. Submit the five architecture artefacts above. Include a duplicate settlement, a broker outage and a warehouse outage in your review.',
['One defensible proposal keeps Orders and Payments in a modular application, gives Inventory a clear interface, and uses durable jobs or an outbox for asynchronous effects. Extract Inventory only if team ownership or measured workload justifies it. Use a broker with retention and independent consumers when those needs outweigh its operational cost; Kafka is an option, not a prerequisite.',
'A duplicate settlement is absorbed by transactional identity handling. A broker outage leaves committed outbox work pending and visible. A warehouse outage leaves fulfilment pending with an explicit reservation policy. Define who resolves old pending cases, how users see them and what evidence would trigger the next architectural change.'],
['The simplest viable design is compared fairly.','Failures have durable states and accountable owners.','Future extraction has an evidence-based trigger.'],['MICRO','SRE'])
assert len(CHAPTERS)==24
